#!markdown

## Drop-Down List of Clients (and Yards)

 Below we have an inner-join of clients and yards, because every result has to fullfill all conditions from lines 2 -> 7

#!csharp

var yardsInSupplier = Given<Supplier>.Match((supplier, facts) =>
  from client in facts.OfType<Client>()
  where client.supplier == supplier && !client.IsDeleted
  from yardClients in facts.OfType<YardClients>()
  where yardClients.client == client && !yardClients.IsDeleted
  from yard in facts.OfType<Yard>()
  where yard.supplier == supplier && !yard.IsDeleted && yard == yardClients.yard

  select new
  {
    clientNames = client.Names.Select(name => name.value),
    yardNames = yard.Names.Select(name => name.value),
    yardAddresses = yard.Addresses.Select(address => new
      {
        street = address.street,
        number = address.number,
        postalCode = address.postalCode,
        city = address.place,
        country = address.country
      })
  }
);

var yards = await jinagaClient.Query(yardsInSupplier, supplier);

var yardViewModels =
  from yard in yards
  // let clientName = yard.clientNames.FirstOrDefault()
  let yardName = yard.yardNames.FirstOrDefault()
  let yardAddress = yard.yardAddresses.FirstOrDefault()
  select new
  {
    yard,
    // clientName = clientName ?? "Unknown client",
    yardName = yardName ?? "Unknown yard",
    street = yardAddress?.street ?? "Unknown street",
    number = yardAddress?.number ?? "Unknown number",
    postalCode = yardAddress?.postalCode ?? "Unknown postal code",
    city = yardAddress?.city ?? "Unknown city",
    country = yardAddress?.country ?? "Unknown country"
  };

yardViewModels

#!markdown

Below we see all clients, even the ones without yards (eg client D).  But we only see one yard per client and we do not see yards that have no client.

#!csharp

var clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin = Given<Supplier>.Match((supplier, facts) =>
  from client in facts.OfType<Client>()
  where client.supplier == supplier && !client.IsDeleted
  select new {
    clientNames = client.Names.Select(name => name.value),
    yards = from yardClients in facts.OfType<YardClients>()
            where yardClients.client == client && !yardClients.IsDeleted
            from yard in facts.OfType<Yard>()
            where yard.supplier == supplier && !yard.IsDeleted && yard == yardClients.yard
            select new
            {                
                yardNames = yard.Names.Select(name => name.value),
                yardAddresses = yard.Addresses.Select(address => new
                {
                    street = address.street,
                    number = address.number,
                    postalCode = address.postalCode,
                    city = address.place,
                    country = address.country
                })
            }
  }   
);


var clients = await jinagaClient.Query(clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin, supplier);

var clientViewModels =
  from client in clients
  // let clientName = yard.clientNames.FirstOrDefault()
  let yard = client.yards.FirstOrDefault()
  let yardName = yard?.yardNames.FirstOrDefault()
  let yardAddress = yard?.yardAddresses.FirstOrDefault()
  select new
  {
    client,
    // clientName = clientName ?? "Unknown client",
    yardName = yardName ?? "Unknown yard",
    street = yardAddress?.street ?? "Unknown street",
    number = yardAddress?.number ?? "Unknown number",
    postalCode = yardAddress?.postalCode ?? "Unknown postal code",
    city = yardAddress?.city ?? "Unknown city",
    country = yardAddress?.country ?? "Unknown country"
  };

clientViewModels

#!markdown

Below we see all clients, even the ones without yards (eg client D), and we see all their yards.  
Two problems:
- The yards are grouped by Client, while we want one line for every unique client-yard combination.   
- We do not see yards that have no client.

#!csharp

var clients = await jinagaClient.Query(clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin, supplier);

var clientViewModels =
  from client in clients
  
  select new
  {
    clientName = client.clientNames.FirstOrDefault(),
    yards = client.yards,
  };

clientViewModels

#!markdown

Below we see one line for every unique client-yard combination, but:  
- Client D is missing
- Yard X is missing   

because multiple "froms" at the same level.

#!csharp

var clients = await jinagaClient.Query(clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin, supplier);

var clientViewModels =
  from client in clients
  from yard in client.yards
  
  select new
  {
    clientName = client.clientNames.FirstOrDefault(),
    yardName = yard.yardNames.FirstOrDefault(),
    yardAddress = yard.yardAddresses.FirstOrDefault(),
  };

clientViewModels

#!markdown

Almost there !  
Below we see all clients and all their yards in a flat list, but we do not see yards that have no client (eg YardX). 
This cannot be solved directly in Jinaga, as a tupple has to fullfill all conditions in order to make inverses work.  So there is no OR and there is no UNION in Jinaga.
The only way to solve this is to create a second specification and do a UNION on the results of the two queries.  
  
But a Union of two structures requires requires these structures to have exactly the same type ... so let's first define that type:

#!csharp

class ClientYardType {
    public string clientName {get; init;}
    public string yardName {get; init;}
}

#!csharp

var clients = await jinagaClient.Query(clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin, supplier);

// This query has to be a seperate Linq query.  It can not be part of the Jinaga query as Jinaga does NOT support DefaultIfEmpty, FirstOrDefault, First, Single, SingleOrDefault !!
var clientViewModels =
  from client in clients
  
  select new 
  {

    projection = from yard in client.yards.DefaultIfEmpty()
    select new ClientYardType
    {
        //yard = yard
      clientName = client.clientNames.FirstOrDefault(),
      yardName = yard==null ? null : yard.yardNames.FirstOrDefault(),
    }
  };

clientViewModels.SelectMany(i => i.projection)

#!markdown

The specification to get all yards which have no clients

#!csharp

var yardsInSupplierWithoutClient = Given<Supplier>.Match((supplier, facts) =>
    from yard in facts.OfType<Yard>()
    where yard.supplier == supplier 
       && !yard.IsDeleted  
       && !facts.Any<YardClients>( yardClients => yardClients.yard == yard
                                          && !facts.Any<YardClientsDelete>(yardClientsDelete => yardClientsDelete.yardClients == yardClients)       
       )
    select new {
        yard,
        yardNames = yard.Names.Select(name => name.value)
    }
);


var yards = await jinagaClient.Query(yardsInSupplierWithoutClient, supplier);

var yardViewModels =
  from yard in yards
  select new ClientYardType
  {
    clientName = (string) null,
    yardName = yard==null ? null : yard.yardNames.FirstOrDefault(),
  };


yardViewModels

#!markdown

And finally lets do a UNION and there we are  :-)

#!csharp

var unionModel = clientViewModels.SelectMany(i => i.projection).Union(yardViewModels);

unionModel

#!markdown

## Below we add the YardAddress to the previous result

#!csharp

using System.Collections.Immutable;

class YardAddress {
    public string street {get;init;}
    public string number {get;init;}
    public string postalCode {get;init;}
    public string city {get;init;}
    public string country {get;init;}
}

// At this moment Jinage does not support IEnumerable here, but it should.  So awaiting a bugfix we use IQueryable.
class ClientYard2Type {
    public Yard yard{get; init;}
    public IQueryable<string>? clientNames{get;init;} = null;   //Enumerable.Empty<String>().AsQueryable();
    public IQueryable<string> yardNames{get;init;}
    public IQueryable<YardAddress> yardAddresses{get;init;}
}

#!csharp

var clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin2 = Given<Supplier>.Match((supplier, facts) =>
  from client in facts.OfType<Client>()
  where client.supplier == supplier && !client.IsDeleted
  select new {    
    yards = from yardClients in facts.OfType<YardClients>()
            where yardClients.client == client && !yardClients.IsDeleted
            from yard in facts.OfType<Yard>()
            where yard.supplier == supplier && !yard.IsDeleted && yard == yardClients.yard
            select new ClientYard2Type
            {   yard = yard,    
                clientNames = client.Names.Select(name => name.value),         
                yardNames = yard.Names.Select(name => name.value),
                yardAddresses = yard.Addresses.Select(address => new YardAddress
                {
                    street = address.street,
                    number = address.number,
                    postalCode = address.postalCode,
                    city = address.place,
                    country = address.country
                })
            }
  }   
);


var clients = await jinagaClient.Query(clientsInSupplier_OuterJoin2, supplier);

clients

#!csharp

var yardsInSupplierWithoutClient2 = Given<Supplier>.Match((supplier, facts) =>
    from yard in facts.OfType<Yard>()
    where yard.supplier == supplier 
       && !yard.IsDeleted  
       && !facts.Any<YardClients>( yardClients => yardClients.yard == yard
                                          && !facts.Any<YardClientsDelete>(yardClientsDelete => yardClientsDelete.yardClients == yardClients)       
       )
    select new 
    {
        yard = yard,
// We cannot add clientNames here as it is an empty collection which is currently NOT supported by Jinaga.  So we need another linq query after this.
        //clientNames = Array.Empty<String>(),
        //clientNames = null,        
        //clientNames =  [],
        //clientNames = Queryable.Empty<String>(),
        yardNames = yard.Names.Select(name => name.value),
        yardAddresses = yard.Addresses.Select(address => new  YardAddress
        {
            street = address.street,
            number = address.number,
            postalCode = address.postalCode,
            city = address.place,
            country = address.country
        })
    }
);


// Here we work with Jinaga.Query, which returns snapshots (collections).  
// In .NET we will work with Jinaga.Watch which return Jinaga observables to which we can subscribe and so push everything in MVVM observables.


var yards = await jinagaClient.Query(yardsInSupplierWithoutClient2, supplier);

// And here is the linq query to add the empty clientNames collection.   But so this will be done differently in .NET MVVM using Jinaga.Watch.
var yards2 =
    from yard in yards
    select new ClientYard2Type
    {
        yard = yard.yard,
        clientNames = Enumerable.Empty<String>().AsQueryable(),
        yardNames = yard.yardNames,
        yardAddresses = yard.yardAddresses
    };



yards2

#!csharp

var unionModel2 = clients.SelectMany(i => i.yards).Union(yards2);

unionModel2

